<emu-clause id="sec-tei">
  <h1>Transparency Exchange Identifier (TEI)</h1>

  <p>
    The TEI, Transparency Exchange Identifier, is a globally unique release
    identifier based on DNS coupled with new or existing identifiers like EAN
    codes, PURL and others.
  </p>

  <emu-clause id="sec-tei-syntax">
    <h1>Syntax</h1>

    <p>A TEI is a URI as defined in RFC 3986, of the form:</p>

    <pre><code class="text">tei://&lt;domain-name&gt;/&lt;type&gt;/&lt;unique-identifier&gt;</code></pre>

    <ul>
      <li>The scheme shall be <code>tei</code>.</li>
      <li>The <code>domain-name</code> shall be a DNS name under the vendor's control and resolve to a web
        server. The domain name shall not include a port number.</li>
      <li>The <code>type</code> shall consist of lowercase ASCII letters and digits, and start with a letter.
        The type names the identifier scheme of the <code>unique-identifier</code>
        and shall be registered as described in
        <emu-xref href="#sec-tei-types"></emu-xref>.</li>
      <li>The <code>unique-identifier</code> shall be a single path segment in the syntax its <emu-xref href="#sec-tei-types">type</emu-xref> defines.
        The <code>unique-identifier</code> shall identify one or more product releases within the <code>domain-name</code>.
        It shall not contain <code>/</code>, either literally or percent-encoded as <code>%2F</code>.
        Any other characters that are not permitted in a URL path segment by RFC 3986 shall be percent-encoded with uppercase hexadecimal digits.</li>
    </ul>
  </emu-clause>

  <emu-clause id="sec-tei-types">
    <h1>Types</h1>

    <p>TEI types are defined in the TEI type registry at <a href="https://github.com/CycloneDX/transparency-exchange-api/tree/main/tei-types">https://github.com/CycloneDX/transparency-exchange-api/tree/main/tei-types</a>.</p>
    <p>Registration refers to the TEA community process for adding a type, described in the registry.</p>

    <p>The following table illustrates the types registered at the time of publication of this edition of the Standard;
      the registry is the authoritative list.</p>

    <emu-table id="tei-types-table" informative>
      <emu-caption>
        <p>Registered TEI types</p>
      </emu-caption>
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Type</th>
            <th>Unique identifier</th>
            <th>Example</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>

        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td><code>purl</code></td>
            <td>Package URL, Base64URL-encoded without padding</td>
            <td><code>tei://cyclonedx.org/purl/cGtnOm1hdmVuL2NvbW1vbnMtaW8vY29tbW9ucy1pb0AyLjIyLjA</code></td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><code>hash</code></td>
            <td>Hash of an object, <code>&lt;hashtype&gt;:&lt;hex&gt;</code></td>
            <td>
              <code>tei://cyclonedx.org/hash/SHA-256:fd44efd601f651c8865acf0dfeacb0df19a2b50ec69ead0262096fd2f67197b9</code>
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><code>uuid</code></td>
            <td>UUID</td>
            <td><code>tei://cyclonedx.org/uuid/d4d9f54a-abcf-11ee-ac79-1a52914d44b1</code></td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><code>eanupc</code></td>
            <td>EAN or UPC number</td>
            <td><code>tei://example.com/eanupc/1234567890123</code></td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><code>gtin</code></td>
            <td>GTIN</td>
            <td><code>tei://example.org/gtin/0234567890123</code></td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><code>asin</code></td>
            <td>Amazon Standard Identification Number</td>
            <td><code>tei://example.com/asin/B07FZ8S74R</code></td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><code>udi</code></td>
            <td>Unique Device Identifier</td>
            <td><code>tei://cyclonedx.org/udi/00123456789012</code></td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </emu-table>
  </emu-clause>

  <emu-clause id="sec-tei-comparison">
    <h1>Identity and comparison</h1>

    <p>A valid TEI conforms to the following requirements when being treated as
      an identifier:</p>

    <ul>
      <li><p>The scheme <code>tei</code> shall be lowercase;</p></li>
      <li><p>the domain name shall be lowercase ASCII, using A-labels for internationalized names;</p></li>
      <li><p>the TEI type shall be lowercase;</p></li>
      <li>
        <p>the unique identifier shall be formatted exactly as its registry entry defines it;</p>
        <emu-note>
          <p>
            For example, formatting for a UUID would constrain it to all lowercase, a hash would be constrained to hexadecimal characters, and a PURL would be represented as unpadded Base64URL.
          </p>
        </emu-note>
      </li>
      <li>characters that do not require percent-encoding shall not be encoded.</li>
    </ul>

    <emu-example>
      <p>The following TEIs are therefore all distinct:</p>

      <pre><code class="text">tei://example.com/uuid/62f2cf92-ae88-11f1-a698-1a52914d44b2
tei://Example.com/uuid/62f2cf92-ae88-11f1-a698-1a52914d44b2
tei://example.com/uuid/62F2CF92-AE88-11F1-A698-1A52914D44B2
tei://example.com/purl/cGtnOnB5cGkvY3ljbG9uZWR4LXB5dGhvbi1saWI
tei://example.com/purl/cGtnOnB5cGkvY3ljbG9uZWR4LXB5dGhvbi1saWI=
</code></pre>
    </emu-example>

    <p>A TEI carried inside another URL shall be encoded per RFC 3986 for transport.</p>

    <p>
      A TEA server receiving a TEI as a percent-encoded query parameter shall
      decode it exactly once. The resulting TEI shall be used to compare
      against TEIs published by that server. The resulting TEI shall not be
      decoded in any additional or different manner.
    </p>

    <p>
      Two TEIs shall be considered equal if and only if they are the same
      sequence of characters.
    </p>

    <emu-note type="editor">
      <p>
        I think the distinction between identity and resolution needs to be
        communicated in a clearer manner. Maybe by editing the paragraph below,
        maybe something completely new. The below paragraph is not clear enough
        on its own to be used.
      </p>
      <hr />
      <p>This rule defines identity only.
        Resolving a TEI to an API endpoint follows the rules below,
        where the domain name is used as a DNS name and is therefore case-insensitive.</p>
    </emu-note>
  </emu-clause>
</emu-clause>

<emu-clause id="sec-client-requirements">
  <h1>TEA client requirements</h1>

  <emu-note type="editor">
<!--    TODO new title-->
    This might need a new title?
  </emu-note>

  <p>A client shall use a TEI exactly as received and shall not rewrite it.</p>

  <emu-note type="editor">The above paragraph doesn't have a sensible place</emu-note>

  <emu-clause id="sec-tei-resolution">
    <h1>TEI resolution</h1>

    <p>
      A TEA client can initiate discovery of product transparency exchange
      information by resolving the <code>&lt;domain-name&gt;</code> component
      of a TEI to a DNS query and subsequent HTTP request. A client shall
      consider <code>A</code>, <code>AAAA</code> and <code>CNAME</code>
      records.
    </p>

    <emu-note type="editor">
      In the above paragraph, having the specific records as a requirement
      (the sentence with "shall") seems out of place without specifying
      further details. In the original text it's in the passive voice, so it
      needs to be rewritten regardless. I recommend removing it altogether.
    </emu-note>

    <emu-note type="editor">
      I modified the examples in this clause so they would match an earlier
      example as well as each other.
    </emu-note>

    <emu-example>
      <p>
        For a TEI matching<br />
        <code>tei://example.com/uuid/62F2CF92-AE88-11F1-A698-1A52914D44B2</code>,
        <br/>
        a client would query DNS for <code>example.com</code>.
      </p>
    </emu-example>

    <p>
      The TEA client shall connect to the host using HTTPS and verify the
      server certificate, including the server identity check as described
      in RFC 9525. The TEA client shall then request the URL composed of
      the domain name with the path of <code>/.well-known/tea</code>
      appended.
    </p>

    <emu-example>
      <p>
        For a TEI matching<br />
        <code>tei://example.com/uuid/62F2CF92-AE88-11F1-A698-1A52914D44B2</code>,
        <br/>
        the initial request URL would be <code>https://example.com/.well-known/tea</code>
      </p>
    </emu-example>

    <emu-note>
      <p>
        The webserver providing a discovery document as a response to
        <code>/.well-known/tea</code> may or may not be the same server
        providing product release information. See the
        <emu-xref href="#example-discovery-document">example document</emu-xref>.
      </p>
    </emu-note>
  </emu-clause>

  <emu-clause id="sec-client-discovery-endpoint">
    <h1>Discovery document consumption</h1>

    <emu-note type="editor">
      i'm concerned the regular references to "discovery document" regarding a
      document that is not a response from /discovery will lead to confusion
    </emu-note>

    <p>
      A client shall choose an endpoint from the discovery document which lists
      at least one API version supported by the client. The client shall prefer
      endpoints with the highest mutually supported version, based on the
      SemVer <a href="https://semver.org/#spec-item-11">comparison clause</a>.
      <a href="https://semver.org/#spec-item-10">Build metadata</a> shall be
      ignored when comparing versions, both for precedence and for determining
      whether the client supports a version. If the highest mutually supported
      version is advertised with more than one build-metadata description, the
      client may select any.</p>

    <p>
      If there is more than one valid endpoint option after versions have been
      compared, the client shall choose the endpoint with the highest
      <code>priority</code> value (a float between 0 and 1). If
      <code>priority</code> is absent on a well-known endpoint object, the
      client shall treat the value as <code>1</code>.
    </p>

    <p>
      If a client continues to have multiple valid endpoint options, it shall
      treat the array order as priority, with the first entry placing highest.
    </p>

    <p>
      The client shall use the highest mutually supported version to construct
      the full URL to the API endpoint using one of the two following syntax
      options:
    </p>

    <pre><code>https://&lt;url&gt;/v&lt;version&gt;/discovery?tei=&lt;tei&gt;</code></pre>
    <pre><code>https://&lt;url&gt;/v&lt;version&gt;/discovery?purl=&lt;purl&gt;</code></pre>

    <p>
      Where:
    </p>

    <ul>
      <li><code>&lt;url&gt;</code> is the URL associated with the selected version in the discovery document,</li>
      <li><code>&lt;version&gt;</code> is the exact version string as advertised, including any build metadata, and</li>
      <li><code>&lt;tei&gt;</code> is the TEI, percent-encoded according to RFC 3986.</li>
      <li><code>&lt;purl&gt;</code> is a conformant PURL, percent-encoded according to RFC 3986.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>
      Exactly one of <code>tei</code> or <code>purl</code> shall be provided; a
      request which includes neither <code>tei</code> nor <code>purl</code>, or
      a request which includes both <code>tei</code> and <code>purl</code>, is
      rejected with <code>400</code>.
    </p>

    <emu-example>
      <p>Using TEI <code>tei://products.example.com/uuid/d4d9f54a-abcf-11ee-ac79-1a52914d44b1</code></p>
      <p>to construct the API endpoint <code>https://api.example.com/v1.0.0/discovery?tei=tei%3A//products.example.com/uuid/d4d9f54a-abcf-11ee-ac79-1a52914d44b1</code></p>
    </emu-example>

    <emu-example>
      <p>Using TEI <code>tei://products.example.com/purl/cGtnOmRlYi9kZWJpYW4vY3VybEA3LjUwLjMtMT9hcmNoPWkzODYmZGlzdHJvPWplc3NpZQ</code></p>
      <p>to construct the API endpoint <code>https://api2.example.com/mytea/v1.0.0/discovery?tei=tei%3A//products.example.com/purl/cGtnOmRlYi9kZWJpYW4vY3VybEA3LjUwLjMtMT9hcmNoPWkzODYmZGlzdHJvPWplc3NpZQ</code></p>
    </emu-example>

    <emu-example>
      <p>Using TEI <code>tei://products.example.com/purl/cGtnOmRlYi9kZWJpYW4vY3VybEA3LjUwLjMtMT9hcmNoPWkzODYmZGlzdHJvPWplc3NpZQ</code></p>
      <p>to construct the API endpoint <code>https://products.example.com/v1.0.0/discovery?purl=pkg%3Amaven%2Forg.apache.logging.log4j%2Flog4j-core%402.24.3</code></p>
    </emu-example>
  </emu-clause>

  <emu-clause id="sec-client-error-handling">
    <h1>Client error handling</h1>

    <emu-note type="editor">
      This will include some text from "### Unresolved identifiers", avoiding
      duplicate or conflicting requirements.
    </emu-note>
  </emu-clause>
</emu-clause>

<emu-clause id="sec-server-requirements">
  <h1>TEA server requirements</h1>

  <emu-clause id="sec-well-known-response">
    <h1>well-known response</h1>

    <emu-note type="editor">
      <p>The following sentence belongs… somewhere for sure. but probably not here?</p>

      <p>The "tea" well-known URI is registered with IANA.</p>
    </emu-note>

    <p>
      Servers shall not locate the actual TEA service endpoint at the
      <code>/.well-known/tea</code> URI. This URI is reserved for the TEA
      discovery document and uses the well-known URI mechanism defined in
      RFC 8615. A TEA discovery document is a JSON object that provides the
      available TEA server endpoints and supported versions.
    </p>

    <p>
      The discovery document's JSON shall conform to the
      <emu-xref href="#sec-tea-well-known-schema" title></emu-xref>. A
      successful response shall be a JSON object containing an
      <code>endpoints</code> array of one or more
      <emu-xref href="#sec-tea-well-known-endpoint"><code>endpoint</code></emu-xref>
      objects.
    </p>

    <emu-example id="example-discovery-document">
        <pre><code class="json">{
  "schemaVersion": 1,
  "endpoints": [
    {
      "url": "https://api.example.com",
      "versions":
        [
          "1.0.0"
        ],
      "priority": 1
    },
    {
      "url": "https://api2.example.com/mytea",
      "versions":
        [
          "1.0.0"
        ],
      "priority": 0.5
    }
  ]
}</code></pre>
    </emu-example>

    <p>
      The <code>.well-known</code> endpoint shall only be available via HTTPS.
    </p>

    <p>
      Discovery documents may be cached. When caching, implementations
      should:
    </p>

    <ul>
      <li>respect HTTP caching headers;</li>
      <li>periodically refresh discovery data;</li>
      <li>handle endpoint changes gracefully.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>
      While port numbers are not permitted in the TEI syntax or resolution,
      TEA server URLs listed in the discovery document may contain port
      numbers as part of the URL string.
    </p>

    <p>
      Advertised API versions in the discovery document shall fully conform to
      SemVer 2.0.0 (<code>MAJOR.MINOR.PATCH</code>) with optional prerelease
      and build metadata.
    </p>

    <emu-note>
      <p>
        While clients will request a discovery endpoint matching the exact
        version string as published in a discovery document, they ignore build
        metadata when comparing versions.
      </p>
    </emu-note>
  </emu-clause>

  <emu-clause id="sec-discovery-info">
    <h1><code>/discovery</code> response</h1>

    <emu-note type="editor">
      I do not believe there is any text in discovery.md about the server
      /discovery response that is not covered by the API documentation. I think
      this entire clause should be deleted or extremely carefully written to
      reference <emu-xref href="#sec-tea-api-tea-discovery" title></emu-xref>
      and no substantive text.
    </emu-note>
    <emu-note type="editor">
      <p>
        I'm not entirely convinced this paragraph improves clarity, especially
        coming before the auth clause.
      </p>
      <hr>
      <p>The discovery endpoint is a part of the TEA OpenAPI specification. Unlike <code>/token</code>,
        <code>/discovery</code> is required on a conforming TEA API base: a conforming server that exposes
        a given API version shall implement it.</p>
    </emu-note>

    <p>
      A TEA server shall provide a non-empty JSON array containing one or more <emu-xref href="#sec-tea-schema-discovery-info" title></emu-xref> objects in response to a valid request to the <code>/discovery</code> endpoint.
    </p>
  </emu-clause>
</emu-clause>
